  "problem": "Consider a regular pentagon such that the length $s$ of each side varies. What is the best interpretation of ${P}^{\u2032}(s)$ if $P$ is the perimeter of the pentagon?",
  "worked_solution": "The function $P(s)$ gives the perimeter of a pentagon with side $s.$ So,\n\n$$\n{P}^{\u2032}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{\\text{Change in\u00a0}P}{\\text{Change in\u00a0}s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${P}^{\u2032}(s)$ is the rate at which the perimeter changes as each side changes.",
